  summary: "Islamic finance works inside the Islamic economic system, whose rules derive from the Qur’an and Sunnah, with Ijma‘a, Qiyas and Ijtihad as further sources and General Permissibility governing transactions. Shari’ah seeks human well-being in this world and the Hereafter through six primary objectives and related secondary objectives. Islamic economics is normative, accepts markets and ownership, and seeks equitable distribution and basic-needs fulfilment. Capital earns profit only as a residual and bears loss; rent is for non-consumable assets; the key prohibitions are interest, hazard and gambling. The State facilitates, oversees markets and money, and ensures stakeholders do not exploit each other.",
  takeaways: [
    "Sources: Qur’an, Sunnah (primary); Ijma‘a, Qiyas via Ijtihad (secondary).",
    "General Permissibility (Ibahatul Asliyah) governs Mu‘amalat.",
    "Maqasid: religion, life, progeny, property, intellect, honour.",
    "Islamic economics is normative and aims at equitable distribution.",
    "Capital earns profit with liability for loss — never interest; money is not capital per se.",
    "Five factors: land, capital, labour, management, responsibility.",
    "The State oversees markets (Hisbah), ensures justice and stable money."
  ],
